        <technology>
            <technologyDescriptionAndIncludedProcesses>Semi-chemical pulping is similar to chemical mechanical methods in that the material is first chemically treated and then ground into pulp. It can be seen that semi-chemical pulp and chemical mechanical pulp are both chemical and mechanical two-stage pulping. The distinction between the two is often based on the yield of wood raw materials. The yields obtained are generally 10% to 40% higher than those of chemical process since only 25% to 50% of the ligning and 30% to 40% of the hemicelluloses are removed. The major difference between all semichemical techniques and those of acid sulfite processes is that only a portion of the lignin is removed during the cook, after which the pulp is further reduced by mechanical disintegration. This method achieves yields as high as 60 to 80 percent, as opposed to 50 to 55 percent for other chemical processes.</technologyDescriptionAndIncludedProcesses>
            <technologicalApplicability>na</technologicalApplicability>
            <referenceToTechnologyFlowDiagrammOrPicture refObjectId="4562465a-92b8-5dc0-b302-692e4e067f38" type="source data set" uri="../sources/4562465a-92b8-5dc0-b302-692e4e067f38.xml" version="04.00.001">
